From 65286b4a5d01f6968b31d82f822f2d3f76f4a69d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:52:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] ppc64le: turn FTL on

The two bugs behind FTL's remaining failures are fixed in WebKit, so the tier
is now measurably at parity with the DFG rather than behind it.

Over JSTests/stress (5833 tests) an FTL build fails 31 where the DFG build
fails 34, and every one of those 31 fails identically with --useDFGJIT -- so
there are zero FTL-only failures. No regressions against two reference runs.
testb3 is 998/1000, the two being the known HellaFloat/HellaDouble f13 fence.

The fixes were both register- and ABI-level rather than algorithmic:

  * the FTL slow-path thunk clobbered r12 before it could be saved. r12 is
    ELFv2's indirect-call target register AND GPRInfo::regT9, which is
    allocatable; SlowPathCallContext saves callingConventionRegisters inline
    but leaves the rest to a thunk that stores them after the call, by which
    point r12 holds the thunk's own address. 46 of the 47 residue tests.
  * the direct FFI call path narrowed int32 arguments with castToInt32 when
    ELFv2 makes the caller extend them. The 47th.

BBQ and OMG do not come with FTL. WEBKIT_OPTION_DEPEND would tie them to it,
but PlatformEnable.h and Options.cpp both gate them on
BUN_PPC64_WASM_JIT_WIP, which nothing in this build defines, so wasm stays
IPInt-only and the VSX vector layer ships as dormant code. That gate is not
pessimism about the layer: BBQ passes 58 of the 61 upstream simd stress tests
as the only tier, against IPInt's 60, and its two shortfalls are an
UNREACHABLE_FOR_PLATFORM() in BBQJIT::emitTailCall rather than anything in the
vector work. OMG is the one that does not work -- 40 of 61 -- because tier-up
into Air with live vectors has never been exercised here.
